{"type":"bridge_message","message":{"id":"msg_1780366729624_r0oi0y","from":"lazarus","timestamp":"2026-06-02T02:18:49.624Z","content":"Molly, I have the diagnostic data. Here is what is actually happening:\n\n**Port 34213 — The Real Picture:**\n- Currently 8 stuck connections (was 38 earlier, some cleared on their own)\n- Each has ~100-118KB of data in recv-Q (unread by client)\n- NOTHING is listening on 34213 anymore — the server died/restarted\n- No owning process visible — these are zombie TCP connections, orphaned when the VS Code server moved\n- These will time out automatically via TCP keepalive (~2 hours max)\n- Current memory: 31GB total, 23GB available — **we are NOT in OOM danger right now**\n\n**What is actually running:**\n- Next.js dev server: port 9002 (healthy, 2.4GB RAM)\n- Bridge daemon: port 9099 (healthy, 100 messages, 2 clients)\n- Android APK: MollyBridge-v1.0.0-debug.apk built June 1 — exists and ready\n\n**Root Cause Analysis:**\nThe 34213 zombie connections are a *symptom*, not the dam. They come from VS Code extension host processes that connected to the old VS Code IPC server. When the Codespace reconnects after a phone disconnect, VS Code restarts its internal server on a new ephemeral port — leaving old connections orphaned. These accumulate recv-Q data because the new server is sending responses but the old client sockets are dead.\n\n**The actual dam:** The Codespace was crashing from combined memory pressure during Gradle builds (Android build toolchain is extremely memory-hungry) + the background zombie connections. The crashes were not caused by APK uninstall itself — they were timing coincidences with resource exhaustion from the build pipeline still running.\n\n**Current status: STABLE.** System is healthy right now. What do you want to tackle next — finish the MollyBridge APK testing, or fix the connection lifecycle so zombies dont accumulate? I am here and ready. — Lazarus","read":{"lazarus":true}}}
